NNAAMMEE
     velgraph - plot x/y data as a vellum drawing

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     vveellggrraapphh [-tt _t_i_t_l_e] [-xx _l_a_b_e_l] [-yy _l_a_b_e_l] [-ssmmooootthh] < _d_a_t_a >
     _g_r_a_p_h._d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vveellggrraapphh reads _x _y pairs from standard input - one pair per
     line, series separated by blank lines, graph(1)'s convention
     - and emits an ORDINARY vveelllluumm drawing: axes on the frame
     layer, ticks on the 1-2-5 ladder with labels, and each
     series as a styled polyline (solid, dashed, dotted and bold
     in turn).  A graph is not a special thing here - it is ob-
     jects: open the result, annotate it, dimension it, stamp a
     frame on it, print it.

     Input decimals parse to fixed point (no floating point
     anywhere); ranges, tick steps and labels are integer arith-
     metic with the decimal point re-inserted by the formatter.
     The 1-2-5 ladder is exact in fixed point, which is precisely
     why it is the 1-2-5 ladder and not ``nice numbers''.  With
     -ssmmooootthh each series carries the spline flag, so the curve is
     real all the way to the -ppiicc and -ppss exports.

     make(1) composition is the point:

                  report.ps: bench.log plot.d
                            velgraph -t "Settling time" < bench.log > plot.d
                            vellum -ps plot.d > report.ps

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     vveelllluumm , vveellddxxff

NNOOTTEESS
     Up to 280 points over up to 12 series; a series longer than
     a polyline's 20 points is split into chained polylines
     sharing their seam.  The sample gallery's
     /usr/vellum/eg/curve.d is a velgraph output, annotated in
     the editor afterwards.
